_PDE_ is a `Java` application that lets you run [partial differential equations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Partial_differential_equation) (PDEs) of the form:

$$u_t(t,\mathbf{x}) = f(t,\mathbf{x},u,\nabla\mathbf{u}, \nabla^2\mathbf{u})$$
or
$$u_{tt}(t,\mathbf{x}) = f(t,\mathbf{x},u,\nabla\mathbf{u}, \nabla^2\mathbf{u}, u_t)$$

Here, $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^2$, $u_{xy} = \frac{\partial u}{\partial x\partial y}$, $\nabla\mathbf{u} = (u_x, u_y)^T$, $\nabla^2\mathbf{u} = \begin{bmatrix}
 u_{xx}&  u_{xy}\\
 u_{yx}&  u_{yy}    
\end{bmatrix}$. You may also edit the initial condition of $u(0, \mathbf{x})$.

Famous equations include:
- Heat equation: $u_t = \Delta u = \text{div}(\nabla u) = \text{tr}(\nabla^2\mathbf{u}) = u_{xx} + u_{yy}$
- Wave equation: $u_{tt} = \Delta u$
- Continuity equation: $u_t = -\mathbf{v} \cdot \nabla \mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v} \in \mathbb{R}^2$

In the `UI`, there are two main fields:
- The text input to define the initial condition, $u(0, \mathbf{x})$
- The text input to define the PDE

In those input fields, the user can use the usual operators `+, -, *, /, ^` and functions `sin, cos, exp, tan, ln, ...`. In the PDE input, differential operators are available, such as `dx, dy, d2x, d2y, dxy, dt`, which correspond to $u_x, u_y, u_{xx}, u_{yy}, u_{xy}, u_t$, respectively.

Feel free to download the program below.

![*PDE* app video](/posts/PDE/Kind_of_Schrodinger_equation_yt.mp4)

